{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head 660d8d2fddced4d99435f69a8ee69f9680583859 (local and origin request/checkpoint-500k both resolve there; one commit on parent 316d5aeb; diff is 717+/109- across only internal/kernel/checkpoint.go and internal/kernel/kernel_test.go; git diff --check clean; worktree clean at the head after all reviewer mutations were restored).\n\nARCHITECTURE: the affected layer is Kernel (internal/kernel) per docs/reference/architecture.md. The kernel's stated contract is 'signed, profile-bound verification checkpoints and authenticated descendant continuation, with an optional opaque selector supplied by the host' (line 102) and the internal/kernel row (line 338): only Git storage, intents, and a host interface holding an opaque checkpoint object ID, no local checkpoint filesystem I/O, every candidate verified from kernel facts. v2 preserves each clause: still one parentless sequencer-signed Git commit holding one file, still profile-bound and genesis-bound, still authenticated descendant continuation through scanListedAfter, still an opaque host pointer the kernel grants no authority. The container layout is a kernel-internal cache representation, not a cross-layer contract, and no other layer names it. internal/app, internal/service and the CLI are untouched. Contract preserved, so no same-head architecture.md update is required.\n\nSECURITY: signed-field coverage was enumerated one by one and is complete. The SSH signature is over the checkpoint commit, which commits to the tree, which is the single file holding container marker + 4-byte manifest length + canonical manifest JSON + the gzip payload stream; so the signature covers every byte the loader reads, payload stream included. Loader-trusted fields and their coverage: schema (signed; cross-checked against the signed commit marker, v1/v2 markers and schemas bound both ways so a marker/body mismatch cannot be confused); object_format (signed; also equal to store format and desc.ObjectFormat); genesis (signed; equal to the requested genesis); profile (signed; equal to the caller's options.Profile, so a fold@3 checkpoint cannot load under fold@4); depth (signed; 0 \u003c= Depth \u003c len(sequence), every index 1..Depth single-parent chained, genesis parentless); head (signed; must equal sequence[Depth].OID of the live RevListMetadata for the caller's head, so a fork or non-ancestor checkpoint is refused); events count (signed; must equal the rotation-filtered eventPositions derived from the real sequence); and the per-event payload/attachment bytes (signed, and additionally re-bound by HashPayloadTree against the actor-signed PayloadTree and against position.Tree). No loader-trusted field is outside signature coverage.\n\nVERIFICATION ORDER (the central finding, and a strict improvement over main): main's authenticateCheckpointCandidate ran validateCheckpoint over all cached events BEFORE VerifySSHCommit. v2 inverts it: cheap sequence/frontier validation, then rotation-derived key, then VerifySSHCommit on the checkpoint commit, and only then any gzip decode. Nothing is decompressed pre-authentication: readCheckpointCandidate parses only the manifest, bounded at 1 MiB and by the container length, with the payload taken as an un-inflated slice. Pre-authentication allocation is bounded throughout: ReadFileLimit caps the file at 256 MiB, manifestSize is bounded by maxCheckpointManifest and by len(rest), and the one attacker-influenced capacity (make([]CommitMetadata, 0, eventCount) in checkpointEventPositions) is bounded because eventCount \u003c= Depth \u003c len(sequence) is enforced first. TestCheckpointManifestBoundsCannotOverflowSequenceIndex pins Depth=maxInt. Post-authentication the bomb window is bounded too: the decode loop runs exactly len(positions) times and each event is capped at PayloadCeiling minus its envelope, and that subtraction cannot underflow because checkpointEventPositions rejects any envelope over the ceiling first. Attachment bounds are correct: the reader's 1..128 name-length bound is exactly the gitstore attachmentName regexp limit ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,127}$, and the content accumulator uses contentSize \u003e ceiling-total, which cannot overflow since total \u003c= ceiling is an invariant. Truncation and trailing bytes are closed: Multistream(false), a one-byte read that must return EOF, gzip Close (CRC and length), and a trailing-compressed-bytes check on the source. KEY PROVENANCE: the verifying key is never taken from the checkpoint; it is desc.SequencerPublicKey from the genesis descriptor, advanced only through rotation commits found in the real named sequence, each authenticated by the key current immediately before it, with empty-tree and no-self-rotation checks. DOWNGRADE/ROLLBACK: substituting an older or a v1 checkpoint is safe by construction, because any candidate must still be a genuine prefix of the live sequence and the remainder is replayed through authenticated continuation; a mutable pointer is validated as an object ID before reaching any Git command, so it can select only an object, never an option. FAILURE PATH: every refusal returns ErrNoUsableCheckpoint and internal/kernel/kernel.go increments checkpointFallbacks and runs the full scanHead audit; requireCheckpointFallback asserts fullScans==1 and checkpointFallbacks==1, so there is no partial-trust path. DETERMINISM: verified, encoding is byte-identical for identical state; attachment names are sorted, gzip ModTime is zeroed and OS is 255, the manifest is canonical JSON checked by re-marshal equality with DisallowUnknownFields. Note that DEFLATE output is stable for a given Go toolchain rather than guaranteed across toolchains; this is not a correctness dependency, since each checkpoint is signed over the bytes actually written and a differing encoder merely produces a different, independently valid checkpoint. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIZE CLAIM, verified independently with numbers. I reproduced the exact one-iteration benchmark at the head: BenchmarkCompactCheckpointSerializationAtDepth500000 -benchtime=1x gives 230.6 checkpoint_MiB, 483.7 bytes/event, 4.08 s/op, 858 MB allocated, matching the report's 230.6 MiB / 483.7 B/event / 4.004 s. The arithmetic checks out: 480 incompressible bytes plus 12 bytes of framing is 492 raw bytes/event, 234.6 MiB raw, compressing to 483.6 B/event because only the framing is compressible. That is 90.1% of the 256 MiB ceiling, 25.4 MiB headroom, and the ceiling would be reached near 555k events at that payload size. I also checked the conservatism claim against the live log rather than trusting it: the 5845 real event payloads in refs/seq average 1060.6 raw bytes (median 544, p90 2587), but under the identical v2 framing and gzip they compress 3.65x to 290.5 bytes/event, which projects to 138.5 MiB at 500k events and a ceiling near 924k events; live attachments are negligible at 0.04 MiB total. So the incompressible 480-byte model is genuinely conservative, by 1.66x on serialized bytes, and the 500k/256MiB condition is met with real margin. The bound is now O(state), not O(depth): writeCompactCheckpointEvent emits only payload, attachment count, and per-attachment name and content, and drops the sequence-derived Commit, Timestamp and Signed envelope that made v1 cost 2.2-8.5 KB/record and hit the ceiling near 124k. The ceiling check remains enforced and correct in three places, before the manifest, streaming through checkpointLimitWriter, and after Close, and exceeding it fails the write and degrades to full audit rather than producing a bad checkpoint.\n\nMUTATION SPOT-CHECKS, all restored afterwards. (1) Removing the checkpoint-commit VerifySSHCommit turned three tests red, including TestCompactCheckpointAuthenticatesBeforeDecompression, whose error changed from 'signature' to 'gzip: invalid header' - direct proof that the test really pins signature-before-decompression rather than merely observing a failure. (2) Removing the HashPayloadTree binding turned TestReaderCheckpointMismatchCorruptionAndNonDescendantFallBack/payload, TestCheckpointIdentityAndCachedEventGuardsFallBackToFullAudit/payload_tree and TestCompactCheckpointBindsPayloadsToSequenceOrder red, covering the binding in both the legacy and compact paths. (3) A one-byte flip inside the signed compressed payload, re-signed with the correct sequencer key, was rejected and fell back to full audit. One genuine coverage gap found, reported and not blocking: replacing the per-event ceiling at the validateCompactCheckpoint call site with an unbounded value leaves the whole suite green, so the decode-time allocation bound - the load-bearing guard against a large post-authentication allocation - is not pinned by any test. The delivered code is correct and this is only reachable by a sequencer-signed checkpoint, so it is neither a weakening nor a pre-authentication hazard and does not meet the request's changes bar; but it is the one bound a future refactor could delete silently, and it deserves a regression test under a follow-up child request. Related minor observations, also non-blocking: the benchmark comment cites '450 bytes/event measured from the live Workroom payload mix' with no reproducible source, and neither the raw mean (1060.6 B) nor the compressed cost (290.5 B) equals it, though the conservatism conclusion holds either way; the read-side attachment-count cap of 1\u003c\u003c20 is tighter than the write-side 2^32, so a pathological event with more than a million attachments would produce a checkpoint that writes but never loads, an availability-only edge far outside real payload shapes.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: omitting the sequence-derived duplicate fields is the right and smallest cut, since Commit, Timestamp and Signed were only ever re-derived from the live sequence and compared, so dropping them removes bytes without removing a check; v2 in fact strengthens this by using the envelope parsed from the real commit instead of a cached copy. The deterministic gzip plus fixed-width big-endian framing is the smallest mechanism that meets the unchanged 256 MiB limit while keeping every bound explicit, and the EventCount json:'-' tag is what lets v1 canonical-JSON equality keep working unchanged. One removable item: decodeCheckpoint and decodeCompactCheckpoint are now reachable only from tests and the benchmark - production dispatches on the signed commit marker and decodes through validateCompactCheckpoint - and that test-only decoder is the one that inflates with an unbounded ceiling and sizes a slice straight from the manifest EventCount. Folding the tests onto the production decode path would delete a weaker parallel decoder and close the gap in (4) at the same time. Not blocking, and worth a follow-up. No other fluff: no dead fields, no speculative versioning, no configuration knob added.\n\nCOMPATIBILITY: confirmed v2 is only a cache representation change. Existing v1 checkpoints on disk still load and authenticate in full - readCheckpointCandidate accepts both markers, TestReaderAcceptsLegacyCheckpointAfterCompactUpgrade proves a v1 checkpoint restarts with zero full scans, and the hostile-mutation fixtures were deliberately kept on v1 so the legacy guards keep running. The writer now emits v2 only. Both mixed-binary directions are safe and there is no window where neither loads: an older binary meeting a v2 checkpoint rejects the unknown marker and degrades to a full audit, then republishes v1 under the existing ref CAS, and a new binary reads that v1 fine.\n\nGATES at the exact head, run in this worktree and serialized against the other reviewer's runs, with real exit codes: go build ./... clean; go vet ./... clean; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with zero FAIL or panic lines; go test ./internal/kernel -count=1 baseline ok in 311.6 s; go test ./internal/kernel -count=1 -race exit 0, ok in 112.0 s; the 500k benchmark as above. Main advanced to 06594a3b during the review, which does not touch the reviewed commit. No edits to the delivered code and no merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:dd34fea929947767627d27e70ebe3711e5db552f","head":"660d8d2fddced4d99435f69a8ee69f9680583859","verdict":"approved"}}